  <h1 id="top">Old Trapper Beef Jerky: An Honest Review from a Pitmaster</h1>

  <div class="answer-box">
    <p><strong>Old Trapper</strong> is a mass-market American beef jerky brand founded in 1969, best known for its Old Fashioned and Peppered flavors sold at Walmart, Costco, and Sam's Club. It's not premium — texture is soft and sodium runs high — but it's honest value at roughly $1-1.50 per ounce. <strong>My rating: 3.5/5.</strong></p>
  </div>

  <ContentImage src="/images/old-trapper-beef-jerky-hero.webp" alt="A variety of Old Trapper beef jerky bags on a wooden counter with jerky pieces spilling from one open package" eager={true} />

  <p>I have been making beef jerky for a living for fifteen years, and Old Trapper is one of the brands I have taken apart the most — not just tasted, but compared strip-by-strip against my own homemade batches and against every other mass-market brand you can find at a Costco. <em>[Switches to serious face]</em> If you searched for Old Trapper, you probably ran into a Reddit thread telling you to avoid it. I want to address that head-on: some of the criticism is fair, some of it isn't, and none of the review pages on the internet actually break it down honestly. This is that page. Six flavors, the beef stick line, the nutrition reality, where to buy for the best price, and my honest verdict at the end.</p>

  <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
    <p class="toc__title">In this review</p>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#old-trapper-old-fashioned-beef-jerky">Old Trapper Old Fashioned Beef Jerky</a></li>
