    </ol>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="old-trapper-old-fashioned-beef-jerky">Old Trapper Old Fashioned Beef Jerky</h2>
  <p><strong>Old Trapper Old Fashioned Beef Jerky</strong> is the flagship — the flavor most people associate with the brand and the one most commonly stocked at Walmart, Costco, and gas stations. It's a savory, mildly sweet, soy-sauce-forward flavor profile with a soft chew and thin slices that give up quickly when you tear a piece.</p>
  <p>The soft texture is the single most contentious thing about Old Trapper. Traditional jerky purists want a chewy, dry, jaw-workout snack; Old Trapper is closer to a moist tender jerky that pulls apart with almost no effort. If you grew up eating gas-station jerky, this is exactly what you remember. If you're crossing over from premium craft brands like Country Archer or People's Choice, it will feel underwhelming.</p>
  <p>The core ingredients are standard for the category: beef, water, sugar, salt, soy sauce, natural flavor, and sodium nitrite. Nothing exotic, nothing objectionable, but nothing to brag about either.</p>

  <ContentImage src="/images/old-trapper-beef-jerky-flavors.webp" alt="Four Old Trapper beef jerky bags arranged in a row on a wooden counter showing Old Fashioned Peppered Hot and Spicy and Teriyaki flavors" />

  <h2 id="old-trapper-peppered-beef-jerky">Old Trapper Peppered Beef Jerky</h2>
  <p><strong>Old Trapper Peppered Beef Jerky</strong> is the flagship Old Fashioned recipe with a heavy dusting of coarse cracked black pepper on the surface. The pepper is real and generous — you can see it on every strip — and it adds a proper peppery bite without being aggressive.</p>
  <p>Of the six main flavors, Peppered is the one I most consistently reach for from the Old Trapper lineup. The pepper masks some of the soft-texture concern by giving your mouth something distinctive to focus on, and pepper pairs naturally with the savory base. If you can only try one flavor from this brand, make it this one.</p>

  <h2 id="old-trapper-hot-and-spicy-beef-jerky">Old Trapper Hot and Spicy Beef Jerky</h2>
  <p><strong>Old Trapper Hot and Spicy Beef Jerky</strong> is where the brand shows some ambition. The heat comes from a combination of red pepper flakes, cayenne, and jalapeno powder rather than a single-note capsaicin bomb, and it builds gradually rather than hitting all at once.</p>
  <p>The heat level is genuine but not extreme — I would rank it around a 5 out of 10 on the mass-market heat scale. Serious chili-heads will find it mild; heat-cautious eaters will feel it clearly. The sweetness from the base recipe balances it well, so you don't need water after every piece. If you want the same recipe with more punch, look at their premium Double Eagle Hot line instead.</p>

  <h2 id="old-trapper-teriyaki-beef-jerky">Old Trapper Teriyaki Beef Jerky</h2>
  <p><strong>Old Trapper Teriyaki Beef Jerky</strong> is the sweet member of the lineup — noticeably sweeter than the Old Fashioned, with soy, ginger, and brown sugar notes. It's the flavor that most divides opinions in the online reviews: teriyaki lovers rate it highly, savory-only eaters find it too candy-like.</p>
  <p>The sweetness comes from added sugar (roughly 8-9 grams per one-ounce serving compared to 5-6 grams in the Old Fashioned) and it's real enough that I would not recommend eating a whole 3.25 oz bag in one sitting unless you have a serious sweet tooth. It works better as a two-or-three-piece snack alongside something savory.</p>

  <h2 id="old-trapper-zero-sugar-beef-jerky">Old Trapper Zero Sugar Beef Jerky</h2>
  <p><strong>Old Trapper Zero Sugar Beef Jerky</strong> is the brand's answer to the keto and low-carb crowd. No added sugar, and net carbs come in around 1-2 grams per serving. Sodium is still high (mass-market jerky reality) but the sugar cut is real.</p>
  <p>Flavor-wise, removing the sugar removes some of the balance from the Old Fashioned base recipe — the jerky reads a little flatter and saltier without the sweetness to round it off. It's a fair trade if you're strictly avoiding sugar, but if carbs aren't a concern, the regular Old Fashioned tastes better. For a truly clean-label sugar-free option, look at Chomps or People's Choice No Sugar Old Fashioned instead.</p>

  <h2 id="old-trapper-double-eagle-beef-jerky">Old Trapper Double Eagle Beef Jerky</h2>
  <p><strong>Old Trapper Double Eagle Beef Jerky</strong> is Old Trapper's premium tier — thicker slices, larger pieces, and a firmer chew than the standard line. If the standard Old Trapper feels too soft to you, Double Eagle is the version to try. The pieces are noticeably bigger and closer to what most people picture when they hear "traditional beef jerky."</p>
  <p>The trade-off is price and availability. Double Eagle costs roughly 30-40% more per ounce than the standard line and shows up less consistently at retailers — you're more likely to find it at Sam's Club and specialty jerky sites than at your local Walmart.</p>
  <p><strong>A quick sidebar on the "Old Trapper Double Eagle coins":</strong> the coins (or medallions) are a separate brand collectible, not a jerky product. They're novelty tokens issued by Old Trapper as promotional or commemorative pieces. If you searched for the coins expecting jerky, you're in the right place for the jerky but on the wrong page for the coins.</p>

  <ContentImage src="/images/old-trapper-double-eagle.webp" alt="Old Trapper Double Eagle beef jerky package with thicker larger pieces of jerky spilling out onto a wooden board" />

  <h2 id="old-trapper-beef-sticks">Old Trapper Beef Sticks</h2>
  <p>Old Trapper's product line extends well beyond flat beef jerky. The <strong>Old Trapper Beef Sticks</strong> line is a legitimate parallel product category and worth its own coverage:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Old Trapper Beef Sticks (regular):</strong> The classic Slim-Jim-shaped snack stick. Softer than jerky, saltier, and a lot easier to eat while driving.</li>
    <li><strong>Old Trapper Deli Style Beef Sticks:</strong> Larger diameter, sold in Costco-style multi-packs. Same recipe, more meat per stick.</li>
    <li><strong>Old Trapper Jalapeno Beef Sticks:</strong> Real pickled-jalapeno pieces embedded in the meat. Actual heat, not just marketing.</li>
    <li><strong>Old Trapper Pepperoni Sticks:</strong> A pepperoni-styled variant with Italian seasoning. Not authentic pepperoni, but the flavor direction is there.</li>
    <li><strong>Old Trapper Kippered Beef:</strong> A distinct product — moister, thicker, chunk-cut rather than sliced. Somewhere between a jerky and a wet-cured beef snack.</li>
    <li><strong>Old Trapper Jerky Rounds:</strong> Coin-shaped ground-beef jerky pieces. Softer than the sliced whole-muscle jerky, more of a snack than a jerky purist would recognize.</li>
  </ul>

  <ContentImage src="/images/old-trapper-beef-sticks.webp" alt="A lineup of Old Trapper beef sticks including regular beef sticks Deli Style pepperoni sticks and jalapeno beef sticks arranged in a row" />

  <p>The Kippered Beef is the standout in this side of the product line — genuinely different from what most brands make, and the closest thing Old Trapper has to a "regional specialty" product. If you're bored of standard flat jerky, try it.</p>

  <h2 id="is-old-trapper-beef-jerky-gluten-free">Is Old Trapper Beef Jerky Gluten Free</h2>
  <p><strong>Is Old Trapper beef jerky gluten free?</strong> Yes — the core Old Trapper beef jerky flavors (Old Fashioned, Peppered, Hot and Spicy, Teriyaki, Zero Sugar) do not contain wheat, barley, or rye ingredients, and the brand states this clearly on its packaging. The Double Eagle line is also gluten-free.</p>
  <p>Important caveats:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Not third-party certified gluten-free.</strong> Old Trapper does not carry the Certified Gluten-Free logo from GFCO or similar organizations. If you have celiac disease and need the certification, look at brands like Chomps or Krave that carry the third-party certification.</li>
    <li><strong>Shared facility.</strong> Old Trapper's manufacturing facility processes multiple products; cross-contact with gluten-containing products is not explicitly disclosed. Most gluten-sensitive eaters tolerate Old Trapper without issue; celiac disease sufferers should verify the specific product's current label.</li>
    <li><strong>Beef Sticks are also gluten-free</strong>, though the ingredient list is longer and includes more binders. Check the label of the specific stick product you buy.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Verify current label information at <a href="https://oldtrapper.com/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">oldtrapper.com</a> before eating if you're strict about gluten — recipes can change without notice.</p>

  <h2 id="old-trapper-beef-jerky-nutrition">Old Trapper Beef Jerky Nutrition</h2>
  <p><strong>Old Trapper beef jerky nutrition</strong> per one-ounce (28g) serving of the Old Fashioned flavor:</p>
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Nutrient</th><th>Per 1 oz serving</th><th>% Daily Value</th></tr>
    </thead>
