      <tr><td>Sugars (incl. added)</td><td>5 g</td><td>—</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Protein</strong></td><td><strong>11 g</strong></td><td>22%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The two numbers that matter most: <strong>460 mg of sodium</strong> per one-ounce serving (a standard 3.25 oz bag delivers ~1,500 mg, roughly 65% of the <a href="https://www.heart.org/en/healthy-living/healthy-eating/eat-smart/sodium/how-much-sodium-should-i-eat-per-day" rel="noopener" target="_blank">American Heart Association's daily 2,300 mg ceiling</a>), and <strong>5 g of added sugar</strong> per ounce. Protein is solid at 11 grams. Flavor variations shift these numbers by ±20% — the Teriyaki has more sugar, the Zero Sugar has more sodium, and so on.</p>

  <ContentImage src="/images/old-trapper-beef-jerky-nutrition-label.webp" alt="Close up of an Old Trapper beef jerky nutrition facts label showing sodium 460 mg and 11 grams of protein per one ounce serving" />

  <h2 id="is-old-trapper-beef-jerky-healthy">Is Old Trapper Beef Jerky Healthy</h2>
  <p><strong>Is Old Trapper beef jerky healthy?</strong> Depends on what you mean by healthy. If "healthy" means "good source of protein without a lot of fat," yes — 11 grams of protein at 80 calories per ounce is a legitimate high-protein snack. If "healthy" means "low sodium and low sugar," no — Old Trapper is on the higher end for both compared to premium brands like Country Archer or People's Choice.</p>
  <p>Here's the honest breakdown by criterion:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Protein density:</strong> Excellent. 11 g per 80 calories is one of the best protein-to-calorie ratios in packaged snacks.</li>
    <li><strong>Sodium:</strong> Poor. 460 mg per serving is on the high side even for cured meat. For context, our <a href="/low-sodium-beef-jerky/">low sodium beef jerky guide</a> covers brands under 200 mg per ounce if sodium is a concern.</li>
    <li><strong>Added sugar:</strong> Moderate to high depending on flavor. Zero Sugar version exists.</li>
    <li><strong>Fat:</strong> Very low — this is a legitimate low-fat protein source.</li>
    <li><strong>Preservatives:</strong> Contains sodium nitrite (standard curing salt) and natural flavors. Not clean-label but not artificial either.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For most eaters, Old Trapper as an occasional snack fits fine into a normal diet. If you're actively managing blood pressure or on a low-sodium prescription diet, look at genuinely low-sodium alternatives instead.</p>

  <h2 id="where-to-buy-old-trapper-beef-jerky">Where to Buy Old Trapper Beef Jerky</h2>
  <p><strong>Where to buy Old Trapper beef jerky</strong> — the honest breakdown of where it's cheapest and where it's most reliably stocked:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Costco:</strong> Best price per ounce, particularly on the 18-oz bulk Old Fashioned bags. Selection rotates and Double Eagle is inconsistent. If you eat Old Trapper regularly, this is your default.</li>
    <li><strong>Sam's Club:</strong> Similar bulk-pricing structure to Costco, sometimes with better selection on the Double Eagle line.</li>
    <li><strong>Walmart:</strong> Widest selection of individual 3.25-oz bags across all flavors. Prices are honest mass-market. Best for trying different flavors without buying bulk.</li>
    <li><strong>Amazon:</strong> Every SKU including the Beef Sticks and Kippered Beef lines. Subscribe-and-save works if you have a favorite flavor. Prices are competitive but rarely beat Costco per ounce.</li>
    <li><strong>7-Eleven and gas stations:</strong> Individual bags at the highest per-ounce price you'll pay anywhere. Fine for emergencies, not a rotation choice.</li>
    <li><strong>oldtrapper.com direct:</strong> Every product plus bundles the retailers don't carry. Better for gift purposes and hard-to-find flavors than everyday buying.</li>
  </ul>

  <ContentImage src="/images/old-trapper-beef-jerky-at-costco.webp" alt="Old Trapper beef jerky 18 ounce bulk bag on a Costco warehouse shelf showing the price-per-ounce advantage of buying in bulk" />

  <h2 id="is-old-trapper-beef-jerky-good">Is Old Trapper Beef Jerky Good</h2>
  <p>Here's the honest verdict. <strong>Is Old Trapper beef jerky good?</strong> Yes — but you have to be clear about what you're grading against.</p>
  <p><strong>Where Old Trapper wins:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Widely available — you can find it at almost any US grocery, warehouse club, or convenience store</li>
    <li>Honest value at $1-1.50 per ounce, cheaper in bulk at Costco</li>
    <li>Real whole-muscle beef, not mechanically formed or pressed jerky</li>
    <li>Legitimate American brand with 50+ year history</li>
    <li>Six recognizable flavors covering most palates</li>
    <li>Genuinely gluten-free (though not third-party certified)</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Where it falls short:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Soft texture — not a chew-heavy traditional jerky experience</li>
    <li>High sodium (460 mg per oz) and added sugar in most flavors</li>
    <li>Standard commercial preservatives, not a clean-label product</li>
    <li>Flavors are safe rather than distinctive — nothing here you'll write home about</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Now the elephant in the room: the <strong>Reddit "avoid Old Trapper" thread</strong> that ranks #3 for the brand's own name. The specific complaints from that community are mostly the ones I listed above — soft texture, high sodium, added sugar, "not real jerky." All of those complaints are factually accurate. Where I part ways with the Reddit consensus is on the framing: Old Trapper isn't trying to be premium craft jerky. It's mass-market convenience jerky at mass-market prices, and it's honest about that. Calling it "trash" is like calling McDonald's a bad burger — it's grading a product against a category it doesn't compete in.</p>
  <p><strong>My honest overall rating: 3.5 out of 5.</strong> If you want soft, savory, gas-station-style jerky at $1.20 per ounce that you can find literally anywhere, Old Trapper delivers. If you want serious chewy craft jerky, spend the extra money on Country Archer, People's Choice, Chomps — or better yet, learn to <a href="/how-to-make-beef-jerky/">make beef jerky at home</a> and pick your own <a href="/best-meat-for-beef-jerky/">meat cuts</a>. Both approaches are legitimate; they're just different products.</p>

